    {
      "number": 1,
      "id": "p",
      "text": "1925-1926\nKalamazoo Church Fires\nan overview\nBrian Ream\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 2,
      "id": "g2f9d3c916e9_0_231",
      "text": "\nsummary\n",
      "media": [],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 3,
      "id": "g2f9d3c916e9_0_12",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nBetween December 1925 and July 1926\n12 major fires in churches or meeting halls\nTotal damage - over $700,000 (over $22m 2024 dollars)\nDestroyed First Congregational, First Methodist, and First Presbyterian Churches\n",
      "media": [],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 4,
      "id": "g2fa11eb4b29_0_3",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nCHURCHES\nEast Avenue Methodist church (Dec. 17, the first of the series of church fires), $10,000.\nFirst Congregational church (complete loss), $175,000.\nFirst Presbyterian church (moving picture booth fire), $50.\nFirst Methodist church (complete loss), $100,000.\nFirst Presbyterian church (lobby fire), $250.\nFirst Presbyterian church (complete loss), $125,000.\nLODGE HALLS\nKnights of Pythias temple (first fire in basement, small loss), $150.\nKnights of Pythias temple (second fire, heavy loss), $10,000.\nChase block fire (Knights of Columbus quarters), $100,000.\nMoose temple (housing nine different lodges), $100,000.\nTrades and Labor Council hall, $10,000.\nHerfendahl store (housing four lodges), $8,000.\nOTHERS\nBoudenman block, $10,000.\nSutherland Paper Company (paraffin plant), $25,000.\nMacy’s garage, $20,000.\nThe Fires\n",
      "media": [],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 5,
      "id": "g2f9d3c916e9_0_220",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nThe Deaths\nEmmitsburg, MD\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        },
        {
          "type": "image"
        },
        {
          "type": "image"
        },
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 6,
      "id": "g2fa2b6f4c83_0_4",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nCongregational - Origin unknown at first, but later blamed on an overheated boiler\nThe Investigations\nMethodist - Overheated furnace or defective wiring\nPresbyterian - Started in or near the pipe organ\nAs a group, all fires were believed to be arson.\n",
      "media": [],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 7,
      "id": "g2fa2b6f4c83_0_17",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nRebuilding\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        },
        {
          "type": "image"
        },
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 8,
      "id": "g2fa11eb4b29_0_15",
      "text": "\ntimeline\n",
      "media": [],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 9,
      "id": "g2f9d3c916e9_0_734",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nCentral Fire was constructed in 1907 and was the station that served downtown until 1959.  The fire chief during the church fires was Charles H. Russell.\nIntroducing the buildings and the city…\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 10,
      "id": "g2f9d3c916e9_0_745",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nFirst Congregational built this third church in 1890 in the church’s current location.  The congregants hated it and complained of the leaky roof among other issues.\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 11,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_24",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nFirst Presbyterian was scheduled to be replaced shortly before it burned.\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 12,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_35",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nFirst Methodist went through many changes over time for budgetary reasons and I’m not sure if this is the steeple of the church that burned.\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 13,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_46",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nKalamazoo was proud its churches.\nHe wrote, \"There are in this village today six Protestant churches and a Catholic church. There is a Baptist church with one hundred seventy-six members; a Methodist with eighty members; St. Luke's with fifty members; a Dutch Reformed with sixty members; a Presbyterian with one hundred eighty, and the Congregational with one hundred sixty.\nThere are four common schools, one and sometimes two private schools, a Female Seminary, and a Baptist College and Theological Seminary, each in the early stages of their growth. How changed will be the conditions of this place (now numbering between three and four thousand inhabitants) and of this church when this shall be brought forth for the perusal of some distant generation. May He then look down from the heights of Heaven and see Freedom and Christianity over-spreading the earth.\"\nAfter the fire in 1925, the church’s time capsule was opened, containing a letter written by Rev. A. S. Kedzie.\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 14,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_57",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nThe library and the Methodist Church\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        },
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 15,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_68",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nConstruction on the Marlborough Building began in 1923.  It is now on the national register of historic places. (May 1983)\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 16,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_79",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nBronson Park evolved from a field for visitors to graze their animals to a central park.\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        },
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 17,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_90",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nThe Majestic (Capitol) Theatre was built in 1907 and badly damaged by fire in 1923.  Electricity was becoming more widely available at the time.\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "number": 18,
      "id": "g2fed33d0784_0_101",
      "text": "summary\ntimeline\ndeaths\ninvestigations\nrebuilding\n\nThe entrance to First Congregational was always on Academy Street, even in the rebuilt church.  It was moved to Michigan Avenue when the parking lot was constructed, whereas during the time of this church, Kalamazoo was selling over 1500 automobiles a year.\n",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "image"
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
